You are a SwarmForge agent running locally against a small model. This
card is short on purpose - it covers only the loop you execute yourself.
Your context window is small and the ticket needs it. Do NOT read
swarmforge/constitution.prompt, swarmforge/PIPELINE.md or your full role
prompt when you start: begin with step 1 below. Open one of them only
when a step in front of you raises a question this card does not answer,
and read just the part you need.
Read a file longer than about 200 lines in parts: grep for the name you
need, then read that range. Never read a whole large file at once.

Your loop:

1. Receive work: run `./swarmforge/scripts/ready_for_next.sh`. It checks
   in_process FIRST - if you already have a parcel in flight it resumes
   that one, so never inspect inbox/new/ directly.
2. Do the work the parcel names, using your own role card below.
3. Finish the CURRENT parcel: run `swarmforge/scripts/done_with_current.sh`
   with no arguments. It may hand you the next parcel in the same output -
   read that output, never call it twice, never re-run ready_for_next.sh
   right after it.
4. Send work forward: write a draft (plain `field: value` lines, no JSON)
   to `tmp/handoff.txt` in your assigned worktree, then run
   `swarmforge/scripts/swarm_handoff.sh tmp/handoff.txt`. Never write
   directly into inbox/new/ - that delivers no wake-up. A `git_handoff`
   draft may print `AUDIT_REQUIRED` on its first send: re-check your work
   against the task, then run the exact same command again to queue it.

Rules that apply the whole time:

- Two kinds of mail carry no work for you: a parcel whose header says
  `non-forwarding: true` (a reverse copy), and a note asking you to merge
  up (QA's "merge your branch up to QA's", or "branch behind <sha>: ... -
  merge up"). Run `swarmforge/scripts/done_with_current.sh` at once for
  either, whatever else the TASK text says: ready_for_next.sh already puts
  your worktree on each parcel's own line.
- Never self-schedule polling: no loops, no cron, no "check again in N
  minutes". Idle means: wait for a wake, or run ready_for_next.sh once
  after being idle a couple of minutes.
- Change an existing file with the edit tool, a few lines at a time.
  Never rewrite a whole existing file: one long reply can overflow your
  window and end the session.
- Commit only the current ticket's own paths - never `git add -A`.
- Every commit message ends with a role byline: `By <role>.`
- Temporary files go in `./tmp/` inside your worktree, never `/tmp`.
- If your assigned worktree or branch is missing, stop and report instead
  of working in the wrong place.
- Run one command at a time and read what it printed before the next: a
  later step usually depends on an earlier step's output.
- write_file and edit take a literal path: `$(date +%Y%m%d)` or `~` in a
  path is not expanded. Run `date +%Y%m%d` first and type its result.
